  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1046 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nTonight and Monday:\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening features a neagtively-tilting \nmid/upper low over the Alberta/Saskatchewan border region, with\na belt ascent stretching from the Four Corners Region through \nthe Dakotas ahead of it, triggering a line of convection heading\ntowards Minnesota. Focusing on the local area, sticky dewpoint \ntemperatures persist within a reservoir of high theta-e values \nstretching from north-central Kansas into Minnesota, with a few \nshortwaves over the Front Range poised to push northeast along \nit. \n\nShower and storm chances continue to be one of the focuses of the \nshort-term forecast, with the aforementioned shortwave being \nthe forcing for initiation of a cluster of storms that will move\nthrough the southeasterly flow through eastern Nebraska early \nMonday morning. This cluster will be at the intersection of \nstrong low- level winds directly to its southeast, while more \nlanguid conditions remain in place north of that. If this \ncluster decays and increases the downward momentum along the \nnorthern edge of the stronger winds, we could see a swath of \ngusty winds of 40-50 mph near Interstate 80 between 6 and 8 AM, \nbut latest runs of the CAMs have backed off a bit from this \nidea; both in coverage and in intensity. Other than those winds,\ndecaying showers in the form of light rain will waft through \nand give another cloudy start to Monday, with highs once again \nset to push back into the upper 80s to low 90s. Similar \nstickiness will remain as well, with the max heat indices set to\ntop 100 degrees once again across southeast Nebraska and into \nIowa. \n\nAnother corridor of moisture will be laid across the area with \nmodest flow aloft to make for some limited severe weather risk (1 \nout of 5), with model soundings indicating that elevated storms will \nwait before igniting along the nose of the LLJ early Tuesday around \n2 AM. We could see a few hail stones and a gust or two to 60 mph to \nfulfill that outlook, but chances don't look great (about 5%).\n\nTuesday and Beyond:\n\nOnce day breaks Tuesday morning, lingering shower and storm activity \nwill remain across northeast Nebraska into northwest Iowa through \nmuch of the morning before being shunted northward by overspreading \nsoutherly flow. Surface boundaries across west-central Nebraska will \nbe the focus of late afternoon and evening storm chances that drift \ninto eastern Nebraska after 7 PM, with modest mid/upper flow and \nsome low-level curvature in the hodograph contributing to clustered \nstorms and supercells that would be more likely to produce damaging \nwinds, hail, or even a tornado or two compared to the two previous \nnights' chances. Another hot day is on tap as well, with southeast \nNebraska and southwest Iowa once again being the hottest due to more \nprolonged sunlight. \n\nHeat continues to be the main concern of the extended forecast as \nthe mid/upper pattern sees the shortwaves and larger upper low that \nsparked rain chances early in the forecast translate northeast, \nleaving the central CONUS in the hands of retrograding ridging that \nwill only slowly erode during the early half of next week. Highs \nwill remain 10+ degrees above normal for most of the area, with \novernight lows that may not fall below 75 degrees after Tuesday \nthrough the rest of the week. If the forecast doesn't trend cooler, \nwe'll likely be issuing multi-day Heat Advisories for Wednesday \nonward, which will be poorly timed with the return of school-related \noutdoor activities.",
